          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have osteoarthritis in both knees.",
          "answer": "Thank you for sharing this. Osteoarthritis is a condition of the whole knee joint, not just the cartilage. Over time, cartilage is lost and the bone underneath changes. The condition is active, involving inflammation and attempted repair, so it is not simply wear and tear (Osteoarthritis, General clinical reference).\n\nThe guidance treats exercise as the first-line treatment. It combines three parts: strengthening the muscles around the knee, keeping the joint moving through its full range, and aerobic activity. Starting under supervision can help, because the right movements and gradual progression are hard to judge alone (Exercising with osteoarthritis, lifestyle reference; Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management, guideline).\n\nThe same guideline describes medicines as add-ons to exercise, not replacements. It lists topical anti-inflammatory creams or gels as the preferred first medicine for knee osteoarthritis, and it describes oral anti-inflammatory tablets, steroid injections for short-term flares, and duloxetine for people with widespread pain or a poor response to other options. It advises against opioids for this condition and against glucosamine and chondroitin (Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management, guideline).\n\nThe sources I searched do not cover doses, which options suit a particular person, or how to manage both knees. A clinician can help with those questions. Useful things to bring up include which exercise program fits you, whether a topical or oral medicine is appropriate given your health history, and what to do if symptoms change.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
