          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is walking good or bad for them?",
          "answer": "The search results do not directly say whether walking is good or bad for knee osteoarthritis, so I can't give a clear yes or no from them.\n\nThey do offer some related points. The guideline says exercise is the main treatment and that pain during exercise within tolerable limits is not a sign of damage (Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management, guideline). A lifestyle reference says cushioned footwear is generally suggested. It also says a walking stick, held on the side opposite the affected knee, can reduce the load on the joint (Exercising with osteoarthritis, lifestyle reference). Pacing, which means spreading activity across the day rather than doing it all at once, is listed as a non-drug measure (Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management, guideline).\n\nThese sources do not say how much walking suits a particular person, or whether walking on both knees needs any changes. A clinician or physiotherapist can help you judge what level of walking is right for you, and whether a walking aid or footwear change would help.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
