          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What about swimming instead?",
          "answer": "The search results do not say whether swimming is good or bad for knee osteoarthritis. None of the passages I found covers knee osteoarthritis.\n\nSwimming does appear in other material. A general physical activity summary lists swimming as an example of moderate aerobic activity, meaning activity that raises your breathing but still lets you talk (Physical activity for high blood pressure, lifestyle reference). A diabetes summary mentions swimming as a non-weight-bearing option for people with certain foot problems (Physical activity for type 2 diabetes, lifestyle reference). That passage is about feet, not knees, so it does not tell us how swimming affects the knee joint.\n\nThe exercise guideline I cited earlier recommends strengthening, joint movement, and aerobic activity, but it does not name swimming specifically (Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management, guideline). A physiotherapist or doctor can tell you whether swimming, walking, or another activity suits your knees, and how to build up gradually.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
